pages, 390 wide, motion on
  ok   pages, 390 wide, motion on: What's New has an entry that tells of the rival (8 lines)
  note pages, 390 wide, motion on: What's New: "NBA My Career: the numbers tell the truth now. Stat lines in NBA My Career look like the league. A starter rated in the low 80s scores about 16 a night where the game used to hand him about 20, wings stopped passing like point guards, points go to one decimal, and every season you play from here also keeps your minutes, steals and blocks. All-Star selections exist now, with two new badges (23 in the Trophy Case). The awards add up too: MVP only goes to an All-NBA First Team player on a playoff team, the Rookie of the Year is always on the All-Rookie First Team, All-Defensive goes to actual defenders, and from 2023-24 the 65 game rule is on. A throwback career plays 66 games in 2011-12 and 72 in 2020-21, like the league did, and a trade demand can no longer land you on the team you are already on. Your rival is on the same numbers too: his season line comes from the same place yours does, so when the game says who had the better year, the two lines on the screen back it up. The All-Star card goes by who really made the roster, a near tie tells you who actually leads the head to head (that one was off in all four My Careers), and the Trophy Case tile counts every award, so an All-Rookie or All-Defensive season no longer leaves it reading Empty. Seasons you already played keep the lines they had. The rules and a worked MVP case are on the ? button."
  note pages, 390 wide, motion on: What's New: "NFL My Career: watch your season game by game. The 📺 Week by week button from NBA My Career is on NFL My Career now. Press it instead of Play the season and the season you just played opens up one game at a time: a scoreboard that fills in drive by drive, your line every game whatever you play (a quarterback's yards and touchdowns, a linebacker's tackles and sacks, a kicker's makes and misses), your record after every game, ties included, the halfway mark, the last game, and your playoff run round by round. You face real franchises on the league's 17 game schedule formula, so you meet every division rival home and away. It is the exact season the Play button gives you, and every game adds up to the totals on your season card. Nothing about your career changes whether you watch it or not. Throwback careers: the week by week view is built for the 17 game season, so the 16 game seasons before 2021 have none, 2022 has none because two real teams finished on 16, and the throwback plays against unnamed teams. The game says so before you press."
  ok   pages, 390 wide, motion on: no em or en dash in those lines
  ok   pages, 390 wide, motion on: What's New does not scroll sideways (0 px over)
  note pages, 390 wide, motion on: the page's guide lines with "same scale": "A generated player drafted the same year at your position. He plays his own seasons on the same scale you do, can win a ring before you and retire before you, and the head to head is kept for good. He is fictional, like your own player, so no real player's career is being simulated."
  note pages, 390 wide, motion on: the "?" lines that name the rival: "Read the News box. The paper writes up every season in your own position's stat, the SocialGram shows followers read off your fanbase with three fan comments under the latest post, and the rival's card keeps the head to head against the player drafted the same" / "MAKING THE CALL WHEN YOUR RIVAL FORCES ONE" / "Some seasons your rival hands you a decision instead of a headline: a flagrant you can answer or let go, a debate show offering real money, a youth camp his foundation wants you to co-host, an All-Star vote his team is buying ads for. Every button prints exact" / "How your rival's choices work" / "A season where your rival makes no headline has a 45% shot at a rival choice instead, never both in the same year, and every choice shows up once before any of them repeats. The buttons move only what they print: morale, fanbase, net worth, karma or the heat o" / "Answering your rival's flagrant" / "Your rival takes you out on a drive and the refs upgrade it to a flagrant. Give it right back heats the feud and usually pays Morale +8, but it carries a 30% chance you are the one who gets tossed (Morale -5, Fanbase -5, Net worth -$100k). Help him up on camer" / "Who is my rival?" / "Read the News box. The paper writes up every season in your own position's stat, the SocialGram shows followers read off your fanbase with three fan comments under the latest post, and the rival's card keeps the head to head against the player drafted the same" / "Some seasons your rival hands you a decision instead of a headline: a flagrant you can answer or let go, a debate show offering real money, a youth camp his foundation wants you to co-host, an All-Star vote his team is buying ads for. Every button prints exact" / "A season where your rival makes no headline has a 45% shot at a rival choice instead, never both in the same year, and every choice shows up once before any of them repeats. The buttons move only what they print: morale, fanbase, net worth, karma or the heat o" / "Your rival takes you out on a drive and the refs upgrade it to a flagrant. Give it right back heats the feud and usually pays Morale +8, but it carries a 30% chance you are the one who gets tossed (Morale -5, Fanbase -5, Net worth -$100k). Help him up on camer"
